7. Limitations & Considerations
	•	Entirely conceptual; not operational or turnkey.
	•	Legal, financial, and regulatory compliance would require careful research.
	•	Infrastructure and cooperative success is hypothetical and depends on local feasibility, energy costs, and labor availability.
	•	Participation and family engagement are voluntary.

⸻

8. Conclusion

This framework represents a theoretical model for family-led support of incarcerated individuals, combining pre-release planning, cooperative participation, and potential income-generating infrastructure.
	•	It illustrates what could be possible within existing legal frameworks in California.
	•	The solar/mining component demonstrates how cooperatives might generate sustainable resources while providing skill-building opportunities.
	•	Replication in other communities or states is conceptually possible, though entirely dependent on local legal, logistical, and economic conditions.


